ent and designed his  own vision for a 50 state flag when he  turned in his final assignment Roberts  teacher said anyone can make a flag and  it's got the wrong number of stars the  grade came back a B - Robert tried to  explain his idea to his teacher who  replied I'll change your gray when your  flag is accepted by the US Congress game  on Robert wrote 21 letters and called  the White House 18 times until one day  in 1960 Roberts phone rang  [Music]  hello hello Robert huh it was President  Eisenhower with news that Roberts  50-state flag design had actually been  chosen as the new official flag of the  United States the president invited  Roberts Washington where he saw his  American flag fly for the first time on  July 4th nineteen sixty cents then  Roberts 50 state flag has remained  unmatched is the quintessential symbol  of America but I know what you're  thinking whatever happened to that B -  Roberts teacher honored his promise and  changed the grade to an A  [Music]  figure one the horse this is a story  about a horse a force whose fate was  inextricably tied to the invention of  film and it all started with an innocent  question do all four of horses hooves  leave the ground at any point during a  gallop leland stanford yes the dignified  chap who founded stanford university  what thought they did all four of a  horse's hooves leave the ground  well galloping but some other academics  thought they didn't one foot is most  certainly always touching the ground so  to put the matter to rest stanford hired  a professional photographer  Eadweard Muybridge to capture the truth  on film there was a bet thousands of  dollars were at stake as well as a man's  reputation but wait cameras at the time  could not capture things moving quickly  there was no such thing as a movie  camera in 1878  so Muybridge had to get creative he  devised an ingenious solution 12 wire  triggers and 12 shutters for 12 cameras  all evenly spaced to capture a horse and  carriage on a ra